> Derive the frame invariant and reachability guarantee

The canonical implementation keeps an explicit frame (vertex,next successor)
for each active vertex. Its stack is the current directed tree path from
source to the current vertex. A newly entered vertex is marked immediately,
so a loop or another incoming arc cannot enter it twice. Before descending,
the parent's successor position advances. When the child finishes, that
position identifies exactly where the corresponding recursive call would
resume. Induction over entries, edge scans and returns shows that this
explicit loop preserves the old recursive adjacency order and first path.
It avoids dependence on the process's recursive call-stack depth.

A returned path is valid because every non-root frame was pushed through
an actual directed arc from its parent. Vertices on it are distinct because
all entries obey global marking. If the target is entered, the kernel stops
before examining its outgoing arcs. It does not pop the successful path or
fabricate finish events during a hypothetical unwind. If the stack becomes
empty without the target, every reachable vertex has been entered: otherwise,
a source-to-unseen path would contain a first unseen vertex whose reached
predecessor must have scanned and entered it before finishing, a contradiction.
This proves the reachability decision, not minimum path length.

Let R be discovered vertices and E_scan the actual adjacency entries scanned
before termination. Each entry is discovered once, each finished frame pops
once, and each source list position advances once. Thus traversal work is
O(R+E_scan), with R≤n and E_scan≤m. The wrapper still validates all input arcs,
constructs adjacency and initializes n states, giving O(n+m) total worst-case
time even when start=goal. Adjacency occupies O(n+m), search state and possible
output path O(n), and optional actual history O(n+m). Only the current frame
count can be smaller on a shallow tree. No stack snapshot is copied at each
event, avoiding an accidental quadratic trace cost.

> Full traversal gives genuine discovery and finish intervals

The next run has no goal stop. It retains the same eight arcs, adds a
parallel 0→1 arc, a self-loop 2→2 and an unreachable self-loop at vertex
seven. Complete traversal visits the source component, not every graph
component. Its result.found=false and empty path mean that no goal was
requested; preorder/expanded still describe all seven reached vertices.
All ten reachable adjacency entries are scanned, including the duplicate
and reachable loop, while the unreachable loop is only validated/stored.

Timestamps tick only on discoveries and actual finishes. For a tree parent
u and child v in the completed run,

\[
d(u)<d(v)<f(v)<f(u).
\]
\alttext(A child's discovery and finish times lie strictly between those of its tree parent.)

The child call begins after its parent's entry and ends before the parent
can exhaust its remaining adjacency. Repeating this fact nests descendant
intervals; intervals of separate completed branches are disjoint. In a
successful early-stop run, active vertices have no finish time, so no
completed-interval conclusion is inferred for them. Missing times use 2n,
checked for representability before graph allocation; root/unseen parents
and depths use n, and an absent original parent-edge id uses m.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dfs_intervals.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t,std::size_t>> edges{{0,1},{1,3},{3,0},{0,2},{2,4},{4,5},{5,6},{0,6},{0,1},{2,2},{7,7}};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::depth_first_traversal(8,edges,0);
  if(t.goal_requested || t.work.discovered!=7 || t.work.finished!=7 || t.work.edges_examined!=10 || t.events.size()!=24)return 1;
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(50,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramStroke line;line.color={37,99,235};line.width=4;
  const std::vector<std::string> states{"unseen","active","finished"};
  std::print("| vertex | stopping state | parent | tree depth | discovered at | genuinely finished at |\n| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t v=0;v<t.n;++v) {
    const bool seen=t.discovered_at[v]!=2*t.n;d.text({-1.5,-double(v)},std::format("v{}",v),18);
    if(seen) {
      const double a=t.discovered_at[v],b=t.finished_at[v];d.path({{a,-double(v)},{b,-double(v)}},line)
       .ellipse({a,-double(v)},.055,.055,{37,99,235}).ellipse({b,-double(v)},.055,.055,{37,99,235})
       .text({16,-double(v)},std::format("[{},{}]",t.discovered_at[v],t.finished_at[v]),18);
    }else d.text({6.5,-double(v)},"unseen; both timestamps use sentinel 16",18,{100,116,139});
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",v,states[static_cast<std::size_t>(t.state[v])],t.parent[v]==t.n?"-":std::to_string(t.parent[v]),seen?std::to_string(t.depth[v]):"-",seen?std::to_string(t.discovered_at[v]):"none",seen?std::to_string(t.finished_at[v]):"none");
  }
  for(int k=0;k<=13;++k)d.text({double(k),.7},std::to_string(k),14);
  d.text({6.5,1.7},"Complete source-component DFS: descendant time intervals nest",22)
   .text({6.5,-8.4},"Ticks count entries and genuine finishes; unreachable v7 has no interval",20)
   .save_svg("build/dfs_intervals.svg");
  std::print("\nComplete preorder {}; postorder {}; events {} = 2*7+10. Target-state counts: unseen {}, active {}, finished {}.\n",t.preorder,t.postorder,t.events.size(),t.work.unseen_edges,t.work.active_edges,t.work.finished_edges);
  std::print("No goal requested: found {}; path {}; expanded {}. Unreachable vertex 7 remains unseen.\n",t.result.found,t.result.path,t.result.expanded);
}
