> Independent controls and scope

Nine new tests pass AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. Exhaustive
controls enumerate all directed graphs with loops through three vertices and
all loop-free directed graphs on four vertices: 4,626 graphs in total. They
compare 70,210 source-goal searches and 17,954 complete source traversals
with a small recursive oracle that scans the original edge sequence. A
separate transitive-closure computation checks reachability independently
of DFS order. Tests compare selected paths, entry/finish orders, parents,
original parent edges, depths, timestamps, target states, counters and
replayed event/frame boundaries. One thousand seeded ordered multigraphs
add loops, duplicate arcs and reversed edge orders. The original minimal
case, backtracking/early-stop/shortest-path distinction, complete interval
nesting, DAG reverse postorder, ownership, graph/event caps, timestamp
arithmetic and native tree-generator guards pass. A 200,000-vertex ordinary
path and 20,000-vertex complete chain test deep entry and finish behavior.
Existing search_more cases verify compatibility with neighbouring search APIs.

The API answers directed reachability and returns its first DFS-tree path.
It does not enumerate all simple paths, minimize hops or weights, or scan
unreachable components unless another source is requested. Discovery,
expansion and completion are separate observable events; early stopping
does not silently turn partial state into a completed DFS forest. The native
report inputs make these distinctions visible without a forced external dataset.

> References

- Robert Tarjan (1972), *Depth-First Search and Linear Graph Algorithms*, SIAM Journal on Computing 1(2), 146–160. [Original paper DOI](https://doi.org/10.1137/0201010); [paper PDF](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~cdm/resources/Tarjan1972-sccs.pdf). The stack/adjacency view motivates the explicit continuations; this API's goal call stops on first target entry, while its complete call traverses one source component.
- Robert Sedgewick and Kevin Wayne, *Algorithms*, fourth edition, directed DFS paths. [Authors' implementation](https://github.com/kevin-wayne/algs4/blob/master/src/main/java/edu/princeton/cs/algs4/DepthFirstDirectedPaths.java). Global marking and input adjacency order define a DFS path; the library retains those semantics with explicit frames and optional actual records.
